Dimensions

What is a Dimension?

A dimension provides reference information that helps us to understand and analyze the facts
It answers Who, What, Where, When, How.

When did most patients visit the hospital? (Using the Time dimension).
 Where are most patients coming from? (Using the Location dimension)
Who are the patients with the highest rates of a certain illness? (Using the Patient dimension)
How effective is a specific treatment? (Using the Treatment dimension)

Why Dimensions?

Make data readable and meaningful
Enable filtering and grouping
Improve query performance

When used?

Always used in OLAP and Data Warehouses.

Where stored?

In dimension tables.

How they are designed?

Usually descriptive attributes
Relatively slow-changing
Linked to fact tables via keys

Example Dimensions (Healthcare)
Dimension	Example Attributes
Patient Dim	Patient Name, DOB, Gender
Provider Dim	Doctor Name, Specialty
Facility Dim	Hospital Name, Location
Date Dim	Date, Month, Year

Healthcare Scenario

I designed a Patient Dimension that included demographic details and used Slowly Changing Dimensions (Type 2) to track changes like address or insurance updates over time.
